Apologies for the confusion, but the package.json file doesn't directly control the theme or aesthetic features of a website. It's a file primarily used for managing the project dependencies, scripts, version, and other metadata.

The theme of the website is generally controlled by CSS / SCSS / SASS files or in the case of a react project, through styled-components and inline styles in your javascript or jsx files. If you are trying to make a site more modern, you might want to look into adding or updating CSS libraries or frameworks in your "dependencies" or changing the styles used in your components.

If you do want to add a new dependency, for example you want to use a UI library like Material UI, you can add it to your dependencies like this:

{
  "name": "ps",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "private": true,
  "dependencies": {
    "@emotion/styled": "^10.0.27",
    "@testing-library/jest-dom": "^4.2.4",
    "@testing-library/react": "^9.3.2",
    "@testing-library/user-event": "^7.1.2",
    "d3": "^6.2.0",
    "framer-motion": "^2.9.1",
    "material-ui": "^0.20.2",
    "normalize.css": "^8.0.1",
    "react": "^16.13.1",
    "react-dom": "^16.13.1",
    "react-scripts": "3.4.3",
    "rss-parser": "^3.9.0",
    "rss-to-json": "^1.0.5",
    "styled-components": "^5.2.0"
  },
  "scripts": {
    "start": "react-scripts start",
    "build": "react-scripts build",
    "test": "react-scripts test",
    "eject": "react-scripts eject"
  },
  "eslintConfig": {
    "extends": "react-app"
  },
  "browserslist": {
    "production": [
      ">0.2%",
      "not dead",
      "not op_mini all"
    ],
    "development": [
      "last 1 chrome version",
      "last 1 firefox version",
      "last 1 safari version"
    ]
  }
}

You will then need to change your component code to use this new library to achieve the desired modern look.